hello and welcome to another exclusive  collider Sundance interview  brought to you by Kia Telluride huge  thanks to them for making this happen  because it means we get to share with  you cool Sundance movies like the wolf  hour thank you so much to the three of  you for being here  Alistair I wanted to start with you  first it's been a little while since  your last film so I was wondering if you  could paint a picture of the journey  from that one to what we get in the wolf  hour I mean funny enough the film kind  of chronicles a little bit of my journey  in a in a loose loosely based way but  you know I was not not working that the  whole time I think I was just really not  finding the right project to land and  then I had a few projects that you know  didn't quite make it and you know next  thing you know it's been a little  sometimes it's just meant to be fall  into place as it does a lot of people  out there do not know what the wolf hour  is about yet so I will leave it to  whoever would like to do it does anybody  want to give a brief synopsis of the  movie the wolf hour follows a character  Jun Jun Lee who is a recluse living on  the top floor of her tenement building  in the South Bronx in 1977 and it's  clearly a bit off the grid and  struggling to just get the basic  necessities of her life life going and  one morning someone starts ringing her  Bell incessantly and she doesn't seem to  be able to go down to see who it is it's  a it's a creepy touch to it I feel like  anybody could sit there in picture their  apartment alarm going off like that the  buzzer going off and it just keeps you  awake at night there's also this feeling  that you know even when the movie takes  place compared to now in modern times  really the darkness around us can kind  of consume us and make us a little  reclusive and stuck in ourselves so I  was wondering is there anything that the  three of you turned to to kind of get  through tougher times just as a source  of inspiration for anybody watching this  really for me I was looking at I mean I  think and everyone has experienced a  level of depression or anxiety over  certain periods of their lives this was  obviously a very heightened version of  that and she's built a really good case  for why it works for her not to go out  and interact with the rest of the world  apart from the fact that the killer you  know this this Sam aspect Son of Sam  there's many other things she's done  damage to her family structure and and  doesn't want to damage any more people  and herself included so yeah I just did  research on depression I don't have that  reclusive thing on my depression  manifests in different ways when I  experienced it I kind of want to escape  and like rely on and connect with other  people but I can relate to that loss of  relevance that feeling of you know where  you're filled with self-doubt and  self-loathing and your art is no longer  relevant and you and you know but you  have to keep creating and how do you  keep doing that when you're getting  blocked over and over again I think  that's something that's really human for  anyone who isn't a tough spot out there  hopefully they have a character like  yours to turn to who will at least step  in and try to help so is there anyone in  your life that you turn to when you kind  of need someone to give you a little  boost yes my my husband my mother my my  children my puppy not necessarily in  that order but yes absolutely I feel  very I feel very fortunate but yes  people I think when people can become  isolated and it can become so hard to  break through  and and leave the loneliness there's  only one thing that could take me away  from talking about movies and it's  bringing up a puppy but I'm gonna  control myself right now I want to ask a  little bit about the casting process  because obviously you've surrounded  yourself with a great ensemble here and  I was curious for the lead role in  particular how exactly does that happen  do you make a list and put Naomi's name  at the very top of it and keep your  fingers crossed  yeah I mean Naomi's in the top most  people's list I would assume but the  yeah I got really lucky that she  connected with the script and and  invited me to have coffee with her and  we just had a really magical afternoon  talking about the character and where it  was headed and it just felt like the  most natural thing for me and and  hopefully for her too and you know we  just set off down the path it's as  simple as that you have a very very  extensive career with a lot of different  kinds of films and because we're at  Sundance right now a festival that can  really make a difference for certain  artists and really kind of change the  industry I just wanted to get your sense  of how you choose projects especially  when we're talking about some smaller  movies that need to make waves at a  festival versus a big studio blockbuster  project yeah it's never that calculated  I just I mean I think for the most part  I'm looking for a character that's gonna  move me and it's gonna teach me  something in my own spiritual growth in  some way it just lifts off the page and  sometimes it can be just a scene that  makes you go wow this this is the whole  part and this is why I have to do it  sometimes it becomes clearer later and I  think we're just always looking for  growth as an artist to try new things  and you know it depends what you've done  previously and  you know it makes makes a good argument  for trying something the opposite of  what you've just experienced is there a  piece of this character you're going to  take with you I like to let them go to  be honest some sometimes actually  physical things and and and the accent  and they're hard things to break  actually because you study the accent so  much and and American accents are not my  comfort zone at all so when you study  and you change your vowel shapes and  things like that and walk the way the  way you walk and hold your body yeah  those are the things you have to really  shake off so from this project to a  really really big project a little  something called Game of Thrones we have  many viewers out there who are huge huge  fans so was there kind of that  connective element to the character  you're going to play there that really  drew you to that I can't really talk  about the character all I can say is  that the show the brand that everything  that Game of Thrones sets out to achieve  it does in such a terrific way and it's  just a great group of people to be  involved with it is a whole new group of  people that said it's very connected  obviously to the same author and and I'm  just super thrilled it isn't a blind  trust in the brand or do you get all the  information I got all and I I'm not  gonna go I'm not gonna get myself in  trouble oh no sir of course yes it's a  very exciting thing to have coming up  and so is this as well so I will remind  you all the wolf hour keep an eye out  for it a huge congratulations to you all  thank you for being here another thank  you for Kia Telluride for making all of  this happen like and share this  interview we will see you soon with more  Sundance coverage  you
 

